# Carcinoma of the breast

- ****Definition**** - neoplasm arising from the ductal epithelium
- ****Epidemiology:****
  - ****Incidence**** - 1st in incidence and mortality in F (HK cancer registry)
  - ****Demographic**** - increases steadily w/ age in F (20% of \>90y affected), F \>\>\> M
- ****Risk factors of breast cancer:****
  - ****Genetic risk factors**** - 5-10% CA breasts are attributed to hereditary breast cancers (e.g. BRCA-1, BRCA-2 mutations)
  - ****Hormonal risk factors**** - related to duration of exposure to estrogen:
    - Parity - multiparous, breast-feeding and early age of 1st child are protective factors
    - Menstrual Hx - Early menarche, late menopause
    - Obesity - increased aromatase conversion of steroids into estradiol in adipocytes
    - Exogenous source - COC pills, HRT
  - ****Environmental risk factors**** - dietary and radiation exposure:
    - Diet - <u>high alcohol</u> intake a risk factor of breast cancer
    - Radiation - prior radiotherapy (e.g. Hodgkin lymphoma)
- ****Pathology of breast cancer**** - arises from ductal epithelium from major lactiferous ducts to terminal lobules:
  - ****Carcinoma-in-situ**** - preinvasive CA that has yet breached the epithelial basement membrane, increasing in incidence due to mannographic screening (becomes invasive in 20% OF PATIENTS)
    - Ductal carcinoma-in-situ (DCIS) - typically unifocal
    - Lobular carcinoma-in-situ (LCIS) - typically multifocal and bilateral, considered a <u>pre-malignant</u> condition
  - ****Invasive carcinoma**** - CA that has invaded through the epithelial basement membrane:
    - Invasive ductal carcinoma (80%)
    - Invasive lobular carcinoma (3%)
    - Special histological subtypes - tubular, papillary, mucinous, medullary
- ****Molecular pathology of breast cancer**** - role of ER, PR and HER2 (c-erbB2) status for adjuvant hormonal therapy:
  - Luminal type (luminal A and B) - responsive to adjuvant hormonal therapy
  - HER2 - responsive to transtuzumab (Herceptin) and anthracycline-based chemotherapies
  - Basal-like (triple-negative breast cancer) - a/w hereditary breast cancers, and responsive to platinum-based therapy and PARPi
- ****Mode of spread of breast cancer:****
  - ****Direct invasion**** - tumour increases in size and invades adjacent breast tissue, involvement of <u>skin</u>, <u>pectorial muscles</u> and chest wall (if late)
  - ****Lymphatic spread**** - dependent on location of tumour, but also metastatic potential:
    - Axillary LN - for tumours lying laterally
    - Internal mammary LN - for tumours lying medially
    - Supraclavicular LN and contralateral LN - possible and indicates <u>advanced disease</u>
  - ****Haematogenous spread**** - skeletal, but potential everywhere in the body:
    - Skeletal metastasis (osteolytic lesions) - lumbar vertebrae \> femur \> thoracic vertebrae \> skull
    - Other solid organs - liver, lungs, brain, adrenal, ovaries etc.
- ****Clinical features of breast cancer:****
  - Assymptoatic - incidental findings on mammographic screening
  - Breast mass - typically presents as a painless, hard, immovable, irregular lump classically in ROQ (60%)
  - Associated nipple changes:
    - Nipple retraction
    - Nipple discharge
    - Paget's disease of the nipple
  - Signs of locally advanced breast cancer:
    - Skin involvement - overlying erythema, ulceration, or peau d'orange chanes
  - Signs of metastatic disease - skeletal invovement, liver and lungs
- ****Diagnosis**** - triple assessment (clinical assessment, radiological imaging, histopathological assessment), reaches 99.9% diagnostic accuracy
- ****Clinical staging**** - from Hx and P/E:
  - T staging - by clinical breast examination +/- mammography
  - N staging - palpation of axillary and SCN
  - M staging - clinical suspicion from Hx and targeted P/E:
    - Hx - back/ bone pain (skeletal), abdominal pain/ jaundice (liver), SOB/ cough/ haemoptysis (lungs)
    - P/E - abdominal and pelvic examination, targeted chest examination
- ****Staging Ix**** - dependent on the dependent on the suspected clinical staging:
  - ****T staging**** - based on radiological assessment
  - ****N staging**** - Sentinal LN biopsy (if clinically N0) or axillary LN dissection (if clinically N+)
  - ****M staging**** - reserved for advanced disease or clinical suspicion of metastasis:
    - Cross-sectional imaging - CT thorax, CT abdomen, CXR, PET-CT

## Management of breast cancer

- ****Principles of Mx:****
  - Reduce chance of local recurrence
  - Reduce chance of metastatic spread
- ****Approach to Mx of breast cancer:****
  - Early breast cancer (Stage I, IIA) - primary breast surgery and regional nodal resection +/- radiotherapy +/- adjuvant systemic Tx based on molecular properties
  - Locally advanced breast cancer (stage IIB, III) - neoadjuvant therapy, primary breast surgery and regional nodal resection
  - Metastatic breast cancer (stage IV) - systemic Tx
- ****Patey mastectomy:****
  - Procedure - dissection of breast and associated structure en bloc (the whole breast, skin, fat fascia and LN of axilla):
- ****Breast conserving surgery (BCS):****
  - Breast conserving therapy - lumpectomy + radiotherapy
  - Procedure - wide local encision (clear margins w/ at least 1cm of normal breast tissue), no cancer at the inked margin
  - Outcomes:
    - Recurrence - higher rate of recurrance than mastectomy (even if combined w/ radiotherapy)
    - Survival - unchanged in terms of long term outlook
- ****Axilla management**** - Sentinal LN biopsy (cN0 diseases) or axillary dissection:
  - ****Principles of axilla Mx**** - for staging and prognosis, as +ve axilla LN is a marker of prognosis and metastatic potential (axilla surgery does not affect long-term survival)
  - ****Sentinal LN biopsy**** - standard of care for cN0 disease:
    - Procedure:
      - Localise the sentinal node peroperatively by injection of patent blue dye and radioisotope-labelled albumin at the subdermal plexus around the nipple
      - Indentify the sentinal LN - defined as 1st LN receiving drainage
      - Biopsy SLN for frozen section analysis for any nodal metastasis (micro/macro-metastasis and extranodal extension)
    - Interpretion - completion of axillary LN dissection for:
      - \>=3 metastatic LN
  - ****Axillary LN dissection**** (ALND):
    - Procedure - level (lateral p. minor) + II (behind p. minor) clearance
    - Indications - cN1+, SNLB +ve, T4 disease
    - Complications:
      - General surgical risks - scars, surgical site infections, bleeding etc.
      - Lymphedema (10-20% risk)
      - Nerve injuries - long-thoracic nerve, thoracodorsal nerve, intercostobrachial nerves
      - Frozen shoulder
- ****Radiotherapy:****
  - Indications - all patients with BCS, and selected patients post-mastectomy (large tumours, high-grade tumours, large number of +ve nodes, extensive lymphovascular invasion)
- ****Adjuvant systemic therapy****

